<Project>

  <!--
    What gets published is not IdeaBoard.Web on its own, it is IdeaBoard.Web's whole
    project closure. Anything IdeaBoard.App references is published too, so guarding only
    the host would leave the easier mistake uncaught: IdeaBoard.App is where shared code is
    supposed to go, so it is the first place anyone looks for somewhere to put a class.

    Before the solution was split, a reference from the published host to the local host
    would have formed a project cycle and MSBuild would have refused to build it. The two
    hosts are now siblings, that cycle is gone, and a wrong reference anywhere in the
    closure would compile, publish, and carry the local identity handler into production.
    This target puts that protection back by hand.

    The invariant is not "IdeaBoard.Web only references IdeaBoard.App". It is "nothing
    reachable from IdeaBoard.Web references a host". That distinction matters because
    adding a reference to a host feels like a transgression and makes people hesitate,
    while adding one to the shared library feels like exactly what a shared library is
    for. The second is the move somebody makes without noticing.

    Each watched project declares the complete set of references it may have. An empty
    set means none at all, which is how IdeaBoard.App is kept from reaching anything.
    References pointing INTO IdeaBoard.App from projects outside the closure, such as
    IdeaBoard.LocalIdentity, are the safe direction and are deliberately not restricted.

    To watch a third project, add one GuardedProject row.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <GuardedProject Include="IdeaBoard.Web" AllowedReferences="IdeaBoard.App" />
    <GuardedProject Include="IdeaBoard.App" AllowedReferences="" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="GuardPublishedClosureReferences" BeforeTargets="Build">

    <ItemGroup>
      <ThisGuardedProject Include="@(GuardedProject)"
                          Condition="'%(Identity)' == '$(MSBuildProjectName)'" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Condition="'@(ThisGuardedProject)' != ''">
      <AllowedReferenceName Include="%(ThisGuardedProject.AllowedReferences)" />
      <ForbiddenReferenceName Include="@(ProjectReference->'%(Filename)')" />
      <ForbiddenReferenceName Remove="@(AllowedReferenceName)" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error Condition="'@(ForbiddenReferenceName)' != ''"
           Code="IDEABOARD001"
           Text="$(MSBuildProjectName) is inside the published closure and may only reference [@(AllowedReferenceName, ', ')]. It now also references: @(ForbiddenReferenceName, ', '). This is what keeps local identity code out of the published artefact. If the shared code is genuinely needed, it belongs in IdeaBoard.App, which both hosts already reference." />

  </Target>

</Project>
